L000407	\section{Density Functional Theory calculations}
L000408	\label{DFT}
L000409	
L000410	\subsection{Methodology}
L000411	
L000412	The spin-polarized DFT calculations are carried out in the projector augmented wave approach with plane wave basis sets, as implemented in VASP~\cite{bib:kresse93,bib:kresse99}, using van der Waals (vdW) functionals to address the weak interactions between the RE atom and the NaCl film. The functionals used in this work are based on the DFT-D3 method of Grimme with Becke-Jonson damping
L000413	[D3(BJ)]~\cite{bib:d3bj} and optPBE by Klime{\v{s}} {\it et al.} \cite{bib:optpbe}.
L000414	
L000415	We consider two types of substrates. The first one is a slab of 6 monolayers (ML) of NaCl(100) with the experimental lattice constant 4.085\,{\AA}.  The second type is a heterostructure consisting of a thin film of NaCl(100) on 3\,ML Ag(100), with the NaCl strained to a lattice constant 3.988\,{\AA} to match the Ag lattice. We study heterostructures of  $n=2$ and $3$\,ML films of NaCl(100) on 3\,ML Ag(100). The heterostructure model is based on previous work~\cite{bib:pivetta05},  where the NaCl and Ag lattices are rotated by $45^\circ$, minimizing the mismatch between lattice constants.
L000416	In principle, this can result in different registries between the lattices, where the NaCl atoms fall at hollow or top Ag(100) positions. To minimize interaction between adjacent RE adsorbates, we use supercells of $c(2\times2)$ periodicity. Unless otherwise stated, in this work we focus on the results for the hollow registry and the D3(BJ) functionals.
L000417	
L000418	The convergence thresholds for the total energy and the forces on the atoms are $10^{-7}$\,eV and 0.02\,eV{\AA}$^{-1}$, respectively. Partial state-occupancies in the self-consistency cycles are allowed with the Bl\"ochl triangular method \cite{bib:blochl94} and a 0.1\,eV smearing width for the Fermi level ($E_F$). The plane waves are constructed with a $9\times9\times1$ sampling of the
L000419	first Brillouin zone \cite{bib:monk76} and a 280\,eV cut-off.
L000420	
L000421	Na($2p^6 3s^1$), Cl($2s^2p^5$), Gd($5p^66s^25d^1$) and Eu($5p^66s^25d^0$)
L000422	are treated as valence electrons in the calculations ($Z_{\rm vcp}$, valence charge of the pseudopotential).
L000423	In these systems the RE atoms feature a half-filled
L000424	$4f$ orbital, which is inert, thus behaving as Gd$^{3+}$ and Eu$^{2+}$ species.
L000425	This allows us to study the effect of the valency on the adsorption
L000426	independently of the multiplet effects.
L000427	The inclusion of the $4f$ orbital in the valence band is not expected to
L000428	alter the adsorption geometries and energies significantly.
L000429	In other words, we expect other RE adatoms with a given valency to show
L000430	similar binding properties, and therefore we take Gd$^{3+}$ and Eu$^{2+}$ as
L000431	representatives of other trivalent and divalent RE species.
L000432	Intra-atomic electronic structure effects, such as spin-orbit coupling (SOC),
L000433	multiplet splitting due to crystal field and, $f \to d$ electron promotion are disregarded in the present model.
L000434	
L000435	
L000436	\subsection{Defect formation at the substrates}
L000437	
L000438	To investigate the possibility of RE adsorption at defects,
L000439	we create slabs that account for the so-called  Schottky defects,
L000440	consisting of a Na and a Cl vacancy. To calculate the defect formation energies,
L000441	we use the total energy $E_b$ of a NaCl bulk unit cell with one formula unit
L000442	to set a common reference for the pristine and defective substrates.
L000443	For the NaCl 6\,ML substrate we define the Schottky pair formation energy as
L000444	\begin{equation}
L000445	E_S^{(6L)} = E(\mathrm{NaCl}^{\star\dagger}) + E_b - E(\mathrm{NaCl})
L000446	\label{eq:ES_6l}
L000447	\end{equation}
L000448	where $E(\mathrm{NaCl})$ is the total energy of each 6\,ML slab and the $^{\star\dagger}$ symbols are used to
L000449	indicate a pair defect in the slab, consisting of a Na vacant site (Na-vac$^{\star}$) at the top and
L000450	a Cl vacant site (Cl-vac$^{\dagger}$) at the bottom of the slab.
L000451	In the case of the NaCl n\,ML, with $n=2,3$, films on Ag(100), the Schottky pair formation energy is defined as
L000452	\begin{equation}
L000453	E_{S,\mathrm{Ag}}^{(nL)} = E(\mathrm{NaCl}^\star\mathrm{/Ag}) + E(\mathrm{NaCl}^\dagger\mathrm{/Ag}) +
L000454	E_b - 2 E(\mathrm{NaCl/Ag})
L000455	\label{eq:ES_ag2l}
L000456	\end{equation}
L000457	where the $^\star$ and $\dagger$ symbols also indicate the presence of Na-vac and Cl-vac sites at
L000458	the surface.
L000459	
