L000630	We find local minima in the total energy for Gd and Eu physisorbed at top-Cl and top-Na sites,
L000631	chemisorbed at the bridge sites (hollow between Cl-Cl and Na-Na) and as substitutionals of Na,
L000632	where the RE plays the role of a cation due to the $6s$ electrons
L000633	[see Fig. 4(a,b) of the main paper].
L000634	Tab.~\ref{tab:DeltaE_geom_6l}, \ref{tab:DeltaE_geom_ag3l}, and \ref{tab:DeltaE_geom_ag2l}
L000635	 show the RE adsorption geometries (see Fig.~\ref{fig:relax_detail} for a description
L000636	of the parameters calculated in the structure relaxation),
L000637	and the adsorption energy values at different lattice sites
L000638	relative to physisorption at the top-Cl sites, $\Delta E_{ads}$.
L000639	For substitutional RE atoms at Na vacancy sites,
L000640	we first assume that such vacancy exists prior to adsorption of the RE and define
L000641	\begin{equation}
L000642	\Delta E_{ads\star}^{(6L)} = E(\mathrm{RE/NaCl}^{\star\dagger}) + E_b - E(\mathrm{RE/NaCl})
L000643	\label{eq:deltaE_6l}
L000644	\end{equation}
L000645	for the NaCl 6\,ML substrate with respect to the top-Cl site energy.
L000646	In the $n$\,ML NaCl/Ag(100) case, with $n=2$ and 3, in order to ensure balance in the atomic species, we define
L000647	\begin{multline}
L000648	\Delta E_{ads\star}^{(nL)}  = E(\mathrm{RE/NaCl}^\star\mathrm{/Ag}) + E(\mathrm{NaCl}^\dagger\mathrm{/Ag}) +\\ + E_b - E(\mathrm{RE/NaCl/Ag}) - E(\mathrm{NaCl/Ag})
L000649	\label{eq:deltaE_ag2l}
L000650	\end{multline}
L000651	
L000652	However, the physically meaningful quantity in this case must also include
L000653	the cost of creating the defect in the energy balance, since the STM images
L000654	show that there are not point defects on the studied substrates.
L000655	In the tables this quantity is defined as
L000656	$\Delta \tilde E_{ads\star}^{(nL)} = \Delta E_{ads\star}^{(nL)} + E_{S,(\mathrm{Ag})}^{(nL)}$.
L000657	
L000658	All species and substrates have preference for a preexisting Na-vac site.
L000659	When the combined process of creating the Na vacant site and replenishing it with the RE is considered, we find
L000660	that the energetically preferred site changes with the film type for both Gd and Eu species:
L000661	physisorption at top-Cl for the 6\,ML NaCl slab,
L000662	bridge for 3\,ML NaCl/Ag(100) and substitutional for 2\,ML NaCl/Ag(100).
L000663	Nevertheless, it is important to bear in mind that with the present DFT calculations
L000664	we cannot determine whether a divalent or trivalent state will be preferred at
L000665	a given adsorption site, since
L000666	we cannot compare the total energies of the systems featuring different species and
L000667	the intra-atomic $f \to d$ transfer energy contribution is not being considered.
L000668	Still, since the Nd, Dy, Ho and Er atoms have similar $f \to d$ promotion
L000669	energies of $\simeq 1$\,eV \cite{bib:delin97}, we can argue that we will find them
L000670	in similar valency states.
L000671	
L000672	
L000673	The bridge geometry requires a significant distortion of the NaCl lattice.
L000674	In the thin films, the Na-Cl bonds are weakened by the Ag substrate through two effects:
L000675	strain on the lattice parameter and modification of the charge density at the NaCl layer in
L000676	contact with the metal.
L000677	$\Delta E_{ads}$ at bridge is, indeed, reduced by $\simeq 0.3$\,eV when going from 2 to 3\,ML
L000678	for both RE species. At the latter configuration, we also observe that the Gd-Cl bond length is 0.42\,{\AA}
L000679	smaller than the Eu-Cl one, implying a significant role of the $d$-like charge in this particular case.
L000680	This is examined in the next subsection.
